The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (bfqq == bfqd->in_service_queue)
    bfq_bfqq_expire(bfqd, bfqd->in_service_queue, false, BFQQE_PREEMPTED);
```
```c
if (bfq_bfqq_busy(bfqq))
    bfq_deactivate_bfqq(bfqd, bfqq, false, false);
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because the `bfq_queue` (`bfqq`) can be expired (its service stopped) before it is deactivated during a group move. If this happens, the `bfq_queue` may be prematurely freed, leading to instability in the queue management system when the code attempts to access or manipulate the now non-existent queue.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
bfqq->ref++;
```
```c
/* release extra ref taken above, bfqq may happen to be freed now */
bfq_put_queue(bfqq);
```
Reason:
The fix involves moving the increment of the reference count (`bfqq->ref++`) to occur before any potential expiration or deactivation of the `bfq_queue`. This ensures that the `bfq_queue` remains valid and is not freed prematurely during the group move process. The subsequent release of the reference count (`bfq_put_queue(bfqq)`) is done after all operations are complete, ensuring proper management of the queue's lifecycle.

